# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
berkeley-principles-2.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 2 ways: berkeley-principles-2.logic.r2 and berkeley-principles-2.logic.r2.3 have "idea_only" and "idea_only → parts_perceived"; berkeley-principles-2.logic.r2.2 has "idea_only" and "idea_only → □parts_perceived"; they differ when only "every particular finite extension which may be the object of our thought is an idea existing only in the mind" and "each part of a finite extension must be perceived" are true, which the first allows
berkeley-principles-2.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S1 is read berkeley-principles-2.logic.r2 and berkeley-principles-2.logic.r2.3 with no modality; berkeley-principles-2.logic.r2.2 as what is necessary or possible: its "may" or "must" does not say which it means
berkeley-principles-2.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered yes, which keeps berkeley-principles-2.logic.r2 and berkeley-principles-2.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "every particular finite extension which may be the object of our thought is an idea existing only in the mind" and "each part of a finite extension must be perceived" are true and "necessarily, each part of a finite extension must be perceived" is false. Is S1, "Every particular finite extension which may possibly be the object of our thought is an idea existing only in the mind, and consequently each part thereof must be perceived.", true then?
berkeley-principles-2.md:1: argue/readings: S7 is read 2 ways: berkeley-principles-2.logic.r2 has "prejudice_principle → absurdity_swallowed"; berkeley-principles-2.logic.r2.2 and berkeley-principles-2.logic.r2.3 have "prejudice_principle → ◇absurdity_swallowed"; they differ when only "ancient and rooted prejudices pass into principles and are thought privileged from examination" and "possibly, the mind may be prepared to swallow gross absurdities" are true, which the second allows
berkeley-principles-2.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S7 is read berkeley-principles-2.logic.r2 with no modality; berkeley-principles-2.logic.r2.2 and berkeley-principles-2.logic.r2.3 as what is necessary or possible: its "may" does not say which it means
berkeley-principles-2.md:1: argue/answered: S7: answered yes, which keeps berkeley-principles-2.logic.r2.2 and berkeley-principles-2.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "ancient and rooted prejudices pass into principles and are thought privileged from examination", "possibly, the mind may be prepared to swallow gross absurdities" and "possibly, the mind may be prepared to swallow gross absurdities" are true and "the mind may be prepared to swallow gross absurdities" is false. Is S7, "And there is no absurdity so gross, which, by this means, the mind of man may not be prepared to swallow.", true then?
berkeley-principles-2.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: berkeley-principles-2.logic.r2 and berkeley-principles-2.logic.r2.3 have "parts_perceived → (contains_innum → perceive_innum)"; berkeley-principles-2.logic.r2.2 has "□parts_perceived → (contains_innum → perceive_innum)"; they differ when only "each part of a finite extension must be perceived" and "innumerable parts are contained in a finite extension" are true, which the second allows
berkeley-principles-2.md:1: argue/unfaithful: berkeley-principles-2.logic.r2 P10 (S7): the sentence has no word of "ancient and rooted prejudices pass into principles and are thought privileged from examination": is it the sentence P10 came from?
berkeley-principles-2.md:1: argue/negation: berkeley-principles-2.logic.r2 P10 (S7): the sentence says "no", but P10 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "if ancient and rooted prejudices pass into principles and are thought privileged from examination, then the mind may be prepared to swallow gross absurdities"
berkeley-principles-2.md:1: argue/unfaithful: berkeley-principles-2.logic.r2.2 P10 (S7): the sentence has no word of "ancient and rooted prejudices pass into principles and are thought privileged from examination": is it the sentence P10 came from?
berkeley-principles-2.md:1: argue/negation: berkeley-principles-2.logic.r2.2 P10 (S7): the sentence says "no", but P10 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "if ancient and rooted prejudices pass into principles and are thought privileged from examination, then possibly, the mind may be prepared to swallow gross absurdities"
